Instant messaging group chat records (2026-10-09 18:33, community operations duty group):
@C-71260 Please take a look at CS-015029, the author appealed that we ruled him sarcastic wrong.—His original comment was, "Your company's efficiency is truly the best in the industry, you replied with a single sentence in three days." After being reported, we deleted the review as "containing a derogatory point."
—The author's appeal states: This sentence is ironic, literally praised, does not constitute derogation, and demands restoration.—— Regarding the review criteria: If the appeal claims are ironic, two points must be met simultaneously before the original judgment can be overturned: first, the literal positive or neutral evaluation after removing irony; second, the misreading caused by the absence of ironic marks (quotation marks, expressions, reduplications) in the publishing context;A side note: someone suspected the issue might be "no substantive ground".  If the sentence itself contains a negative connotation (such as the complaint about "replying to one sentence every three days"), it is not pure irony.—In his sentence, "reply every three days" is a clear factual complaint, literally carrying a negative connotation, not satisfied with the first point.— Accumulated deduction of 5 points, original verdict upheld.—Got it. Also, the DEV-0963 review terminal will restart this afternoon. Don't forget.— Conclusion I note: The appeal claims fall under irony, but the argument is unfounded, upholding the original verdict; The evidence is clear, and the confidence is high.Incidentally, the other side raised the possibility of "policy misread".
